        opts: [
            "The distracted driver's gross inattention was an extraordinary, unforeseeable superseding cause that cut off the freight carrier's liability as a matter of law.",
            "The fallen speaker crate was merely a passive condition of the roadway rather than an active cause of the collision.",
            "The stopped motorist was contributorily negligent for stopping in a travel lane on an interstate highway.",
            "Commercial freight carriers owe a duty of reasonable care only to travelers whose vehicles are physically struck by falling cargo."
        ],
        ans: 0,
        exp: "Rule: While an initial negligent actor is generally liable for foreseeable intervening forces, an intervening act by a third party operates as a SUPERSEDING CAUSE that severs the chain of proximate causation if the intervening conduct was extraordinary, abnormal, or completely unforeseeable. When cargo falls onto a highway, other drivers stopping safely behind it are foreseeable. However, the freight carrier's best available legal argument to escape liability is that a subsequent driver traveling at freeway speeds with complete inattention due to mobile phone distraction constitutes an extraordinary, reckless intervening act that supersedes the carrier's initial negligence in dropping the cargo (Option A). (Even though a jury might find it foreseeable, asserting that the third party's gross negligence was a superseding cause is the defendant's strongest legal theory). Option B is incorrect because modern tort law has universally abolished the obsolete 'active vs. passive condition' distinction. Option C is incorrect because coming to a safe stop to avoid a massive 90-pound road hazard is reasonable driving, not contributory negligence. Option D is incorrect because cargo carriers owe a duty of care to all travelers foreseeably endangered by dropped highway debris."
